  "narrative": {
    "intro": "McKesson has $11.1B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date across 2,058 awards, with an average award size of $5.4M and obligations down 6.6% year over year. The Department of Veterans Affairs accounts for the vast majority of buying activity, obligating $10.8B in FY2026 to date. Other buying agencies include the Department of Justice with $149M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $124M. The company’s work is concentrated in Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $11.1B, with smaller amounts in Drugs and Druggists' Sundries Merchant Wholesalers at $6.9M and isolated entries in other NAICS markets. Historical obligations show a rise from $7.7B in FY21 to $11.9B in FY25, with the current fiscal year at $11.1B. The dataset reports award-value figures separately for largest awards and expiring contracts, and current-year totals are partial, reflecting fiscal year to date information. This summary highlights where federal money is concentrated and which NAICS markets drive McKesson’s federal revenue in the current fiscal snapshot.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are McKesson’s federal sales?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer, obligating $10.8B in FY2026 to date. Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing is the dominant NAICS market at $11.1B. Other agencies and markets are much smaller by comparison, with the next-largest agency obligations at $149M and $124M. Historical obligations rose to $11.9B in FY25 before registering $11.1B in FY26 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What do the recent trends in obligations show?",
        "body": "Obligations increased from $7.7B in FY21 to $11.9B in FY25 before falling to $11.1B in FY26 to date. Year-over-year obligations are down 6.6%. Award count for FY2026 to date stands at 2,058 with an average award size of $5.4M. The FY26 total is a fiscal year to date partial figure."
      }
    ]
  },
